theory.intro:
Sometimes we know $P(B)$ and the forward probabilities $P(A \mid B)$ and $P(A \mid B')$, but want the **reverse conditional probability** $P(B \mid A)$. Build the tree, find $P(A)$ from the paths, then divide.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.95]
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\coordinate (N1) at (2.4,1.4);
\coordinate (N2) at (2.4,-1.4);
\coordinate (E1) at (5.0,2.2);
\coordinate (E2) at (5.0,0.6);
\coordinate (E3) at (5.0,-0.6);
\coordinate (E4) at (5.0,-2.2);
\node[left] at (O) {Start};
\draw (O) -- (N1) node[midway, above left, font=\small, inner sep=2pt] {$P(B)$};
\draw (O) -- (N2) node[midway, below left, font=\small, inner sep=2pt] {$P(B')$};
\draw (N1) -- (E1) node[midway, above left, font=\small, inner sep=2pt] {$P(A \mid B)$};
\draw (N1) -- (E2) node[midway, below left, font=\small, inner sep=2pt] {$P(A' \mid B)$};
\draw (N2) -- (E3) node[midway, above left, font=\small, inner sep=2pt] {$P(A \mid B')$};
\draw (N2) -- (E4) node[midway, below left, font=\small, inner sep=2pt] {$P(A' \mid B')$};
\node[above right, inner sep=3pt] at (N1) {$B$};
\node[below right, inner sep=3pt] at (N2) {$B'$};
\node[right] at (E1) {$A$};
\node[right] at (E2) {$A'$};
\node[right] at (E3) {$A$};
\node[right] at (E4) {$A'$};
\node[anchor=west, font=\small] at (6.2,2.2) {$P(B \cap A)$};
\node[anchor=west, font=\small] at (6.2,0.6) {$P(B \cap A')$};
\node[anchor=west, font=\small] at (6.2,-0.6) {$P(B' \cap A)$};
\node[anchor=west, font=\small] at (6.2,-2.2) {$P(B' \cap A')$};
\node[font=\small] at (1.2,3.4) {Stage 1};
\node[font=\small] at (3.7,3.4) {Stage 2};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.solution_text:
(a)
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=1cm,draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,inner sep=2pt}]
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (3,1.6);
\coordinate (N) at (3,-1.6);
\coordinate (BA) at (6,2.4);
\coordinate (BN) at (6,0.8);
\coordinate (NA) at (6,-0.8);
\coordinate (NN) at (6,-2.4);
\draw (O)--(B) node[midway,above] {$0.3$};
\draw (O)--(N) node[midway,below] {$0.7$};
\draw (B)--(BA) node[midway,above] {$0.9$};
\draw (B)--(BN) node[midway,below] {$0.1$};
\draw (N)--(NA) node[midway,above] {$0.2$};
\draw (N)--(NN) node[midway,below] {$0.8$};
\path (O) node[left] {Start};
\path (B) node[fill=white] {$B$};
\path (N) node[fill=white] {$B'$};
\path (BA) node[right] {$A$};
\path (BN) node[right] {$A'$};
\path (NA) node[right] {$A$};
\path (NN) node[right] {$A'$};
\path (3,3.2) node {Birth};
\path (6,3.2) node {Article};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Multiply along each path.

$\begin{align*}P(B\cap A)&=0.3\times0.9=0.27\\P(B\cap A')&=0.3\times0.1=0.03\\P(B'\cap A)&=0.7\times0.2=0.14\\P(B'\cap A')&=0.7\times0.8=0.56\end{align*}$

The four path probabilities sum to $1$.

(b) An article can be published with or without a birth.

$\begin{align*}P(A)&=0.27+0.14=0.41\\P(B\mid A)&=\frac{P(B\cap A)}{P(A)}\\&=\frac{0.27}{0.41}\\&=\frac{27}{41}\end{align*}$

(c)

$\begin{align*}P(B'\mid A)&=1-P(B\mid A)\\&=1-\frac{27}{41}\\&=\frac{14}{41}\end{align*}$

(a) The missing branch probabilities are $P(F'\mid D)=1-0.8=0.2$ and $P(F\mid D')=1-0.9=0.1$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=1cm,draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,inner sep=2pt}]
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\coordinate (D) at (3,1.6);
\coordinate (N) at (3,-1.6);
\coordinate (DF) at (6,2.4);
\coordinate (DP) at (6,0.8);
\coordinate (NF) at (6,-0.8);
\coordinate (NP) at (6,-2.4);
\draw (O)--(D) node[midway,above] {$0.2$};
\draw (O)--(N) node[midway,below] {$0.8$};
\draw (D)--(DF) node[midway,above] {$0.8$};
\draw (D)--(DP) node[midway,below] {$0.2$};
\draw (N)--(NF) node[midway,above] {$0.1$};
\draw (N)--(NP) node[midway,below] {$0.9$};
\path (O) node[left] {Start};
\path (D) node[fill=white] {$D$};
\path (N) node[fill=white] {$D'$};
\path (DF) node[right] {$F$};
\path (DP) node[right] {$F'$};
\path (NF) node[right] {$F$};
\path (NP) node[right] {$F'$};
\path (3,3.2) node {Defective};
\path (6,3.2) node {Inspection};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

$\begin{align*}P(D\cap F)&=0.2\times0.8=0.16\\P(D\cap F')&=0.2\times0.2=0.04\\P(D'\cap F)&=0.8\times0.1=0.08\\P(D'\cap F')&=0.8\times0.9=0.72\end{align*}$

The four path probabilities sum to $1$.

(b)

$\begin{align*}P(F)&=0.16+0.08=0.24\\P(D\mid F)&=\frac{P(D\cap F)}{P(F)}\\&=\frac{0.16}{0.24}\\&=\frac{2}{3}\end{align*}$

(c) Passing inspection is the event $F'$.

$\begin{align*}P(F')&=0.04+0.72=0.76\\P(D'\mid F')&=\frac{P(D'\cap F')}{P(F')}\\&=\frac{0.72}{0.76}\\&=\frac{18}{19}\end{align*}$

(a)
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=1cm,draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,inner sep=2pt}]
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\coordinate (J1) at (3,2.8);
\coordinate (J2) at (3,0);
\coordinate (J3) at (3,-2.8);
\coordinate (E1) at (6,3.6);
\coordinate (E2) at (6,2);
\coordinate (E3) at (6,0.8);
\coordinate (E4) at (6,-0.8);
\coordinate (E5) at (6,-2);
\coordinate (E6) at (6,-3.6);
\draw (O)--(J1) node[midway,above] {$\frac13$};
\draw (O)--(J2) node[midway,above] {$\frac13$};
\draw (O)--(J3) node[midway,below] {$\frac13$};
\draw (J1)--(E1) node[midway,above] {$\frac16$};
\draw (J1)--(E2) node[midway,below] {$\frac56$};
\draw (J2)--(E3) node[midway,above] {$\frac26$};
\draw (J2)--(E4) node[midway,below] {$\frac46$};
\draw (J3)--(E5) node[midway,above] {$\frac36$};
\draw (J3)--(E6) node[midway,below] {$\frac36$};
\path (O) node[left] {Start};
\path (J1) node[fill=white] {$J_1$};
\path (J2) node[fill=white] {$J_2$};
\path (J3) node[fill=white] {$J_3$};
\path (E1) node[right] {$S$};
\path (E2) node[right] {$S'$};
\path (E3) node[right] {$S$};
\path (E4) node[right] {$S'$};
\path (E5) node[right] {$S$};
\path (E6) node[right] {$S'$};
\path (3,4.4) node {Die};
\path (6,4.4) node {Roll};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The six path probabilities are $\frac1{18},\frac5{18},\frac2{18},\frac4{18},\frac3{18},\frac3{18}$; they sum to $1$.

(b) A $6$ can come from any of the three dice.

$\begin{align*}P(S)&=P(J_1\cap S)+P(J_2\cap S)+P(J_3\cap S)\\&=\frac13\times\frac16+\frac13\times\frac26+\frac13\times\frac36\\&=\frac6{18}\\P(J_1\mid S)&=\frac{P(J_1\cap S)}{P(S)}\\&=\frac{\frac1{18}}{\frac6{18}}\\&=\frac16\end{align*}$

(c) The two relevant paths are disjoint, so add their probabilities in the numerator.

$\begin{align*}P(J_2\cup J_3\mid S)&=\frac{P(J_2\cap S)+P(J_3\cap S)}{P(S)}\\&=\frac{\frac2{18}+\frac3{18}}{\frac6{18}}\\&=\frac56\end{align*}$

foundation.question_text:
The probability of rain on any day is $0.3$.
When it rains ($R$), Liam is late for work ($L$) $60\%$ of the time.
When it does not rain ($R'$), he is late only $10\%$ of the time.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.95]
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\coordinate (N1) at (2.4,1.4);
\coordinate (N2) at (2.4,-1.4);
\coordinate (E1) at (5.0,2.2);
\coordinate (E2) at (5.0,0.6);
\coordinate (E3) at (5.0,-0.6);
\coordinate (E4) at (5.0,-2.2);
\draw (O) -- (N1) node[midway, above, fill=white, font=\small] {$0.3$};
\draw (O) -- (N2) node[midway, below, fill=white, font=\small] {$0.7$};
\draw (N1) -- (E1) node[midway, above, fill=white, font=\small] {$0.6$};
\draw (N1) -- (E2) node[midway, below, fill=white, font=\small] {$0.4$};
\draw (N2) -- (E3) node[midway, above, fill=white, font=\small] {$0.1$};
\draw (N2) -- (E4) node[midway, below, fill=white, font=\small] {$0.9$};
\node[fill=white, inner sep=2pt] at (N1) {$R$};
\node[fill=white, inner sep=2pt] at (N2) {$R'$};
\node[right] at (E1) {$L$};
\node[right] at (E2) {$L'$};
\node[right] at (E3) {$L$};
\node[right] at (E4) {$L'$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Given that Liam was late for work, find the probability that it was raining, as a fraction in simplest form.

A manufacturing plant produces widgets, of which $10\%$ are defective ($D$).
An automated inspection test fails ($F$) $90\%$ of defective widgets and $5\%$ of non-defective widgets ($D'$).
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.95]
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\coordinate (N1) at (2.4,1.4);
\coordinate (N2) at (2.4,-1.4);
\coordinate (E1) at (5.0,2.2);
\coordinate (E2) at (5.0,0.6);
\coordinate (E3) at (5.0,-0.6);
\coordinate (E4) at (5.0,-2.2);
\draw (O) -- (N1) node[midway, above, fill=white, font=\small] {$0.1$};
\draw (O) -- (N2) node[midway, below, fill=white, font=\small] {$0.9$};
\draw (N1) -- (E1) node[midway, above, fill=white, font=\small] {$0.9$};
\draw (N1) -- (E2) node[midway, below, fill=white, font=\small] {$0.1$};
\draw (N2) -- (E3) node[midway, above, fill=white, font=\small] {$0.05$};
\draw (N2) -- (E4) node[midway, below, fill=white, font=\small] {$0.95$};
\node[fill=white, inner sep=2pt] at (N1) {$D$};
\node[fill=white, inner sep=2pt] at (N2) {$D'$};
\node[right] at (E1) {$F$};
\node[right] at (E2) {$F'$};
\node[right] at (E3) {$F$};
\node[right] at (E4) {$F'$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Find the probability that a widget is defective given that it failed the inspection test, as a fraction in simplest form.

In a population, $5\%$ of people have a specific disease ($D$).
A medical test returns a positive result ($+$) for $95\%$ of people with the disease and for $4\%$ of people without the disease ($D'$).
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.95]
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\coordinate (N1) at (2.4,1.4);
\coordinate (N2) at (2.4,-1.4);
\coordinate (E1) at (5.0,2.2);
\coordinate (E2) at (5.0,0.6);
\coordinate (E3) at (5.0,-0.6);
\coordinate (E4) at (5.0,-2.2);
\draw (O) -- (N1) node[midway, above, fill=white, font=\small] {$0.05$};
\draw (O) -- (N2) node[midway, below, fill=white, font=\small] {$0.95$};
\draw (N1) -- (E1) node[midway, above, fill=white, font=\small] {$0.95$};
\draw (N1) -- (E2) node[midway, below, fill=white, font=\small] {$0.05$};
\draw (N2) -- (E3) node[midway, above, fill=white, font=\small] {$0.04$};
\draw (N2) -- (E4) node[midway, below, fill=white, font=\small] {$0.96$};
\node[fill=white, inner sep=2pt] at (N1) {$D$};
\node[fill=white, inner sep=2pt] at (N2) {$D'$};
\node[right] at (E1) {$+$};
\node[right] at (E2) {$-$};
\node[right] at (E3) {$+$};
\node[right] at (E4) {$-$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Find the probability that a person actually has the disease given that they tested positive, as a fraction in simplest form.
